{#-
  Shared MAIN-LOOP Codex invoke + adapt + skip-relay recipe.

  PLAN-codex-second-opinion-sandbox ADR-002/003: the ORCHESTRATING MAIN LOOP
  (the stage prompt — review/plan), NOT a tool-restricted reviewer subagent,
  runs Codex. The call needs outbound network egress, which Claude Code's Bash
  sandbox blocks and a non-interactive subagent cannot be prompted to approve.
  Two layers escape it: (1) a settings.json `allow` rule (`Bash(codex exec:*)`)
  pre-approves the permission prompt (auditable, headless-safe), and (2) the
  orchestrator runs that ONE Bash call with the Bash tool parameter
  `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`. Codex itself stays hermetic via its own
  `--sandbox read-only --ignore-user-config --ignore-rules` flags.

  REVIEW-2026-06-17 hardening (M1/M2/M3/M4):
  - M1: the sandbox-disabled Bash call is ITS OWN command, beginning with
    `codex exec`, so the `Bash(codex exec:*)` allow rule actually matches it
    (a compound line beginning with anything else does NOT prefix-match → a
    headless permission-deny → silent Claude-only degrade).
  - M2: the untrusted diff/context is written to the prompt file with the
    Write tool, never a double-quoted shell assignment (double quotes still
    expand command substitutions from adversarial diff text — under a disabled
    sandbox that is host command execution).
  - M3: the Claude-Code-only sandbox-escape directive is gated on `not is_codex`;
    the Codex runtime gets a runtime-appropriate note instead.
  - M4: the adapter runs only after a status-guarded exit-0 check.

  Caller sets `codex_stage` ("review" | "plan") before the include (defaults to
  "review"). Self-gated on codex_second_opinion.enabled with `{%- ... %}`
  whitespace control so a standalone / disabled render is byte-zero
  (failures.md [fail:design] absent-case rule). `is_codex` defaults to false for
  standalone partial renders.
-#}
{%- set _csoo = (config.get('codex_second_opinion') or {}) if config else {} -%}
{%- if _csoo.get('enabled') %}
{%- set _stage = codex_stage | default('review', true) %}
{%- set _is_codex = is_codex if is_codex is defined else false %}
**Invoke.** Run Codex as a separate, sandbox-isolated step. Do NOT build the
prompt inside the same shell line as the `codex exec` call.

First create the temp files (ordinary sandboxed Bash) and note the two paths:

```bash
prompt_tmp=$(mktemp); out_tmp=$(mktemp); printf 'prompt=%s\nout=%s\n' "$prompt_tmp" "$out_tmp"
```

Then write the diff + {{ _stage }} context to the prompt-file path **using the
Write tool** — not a shell variable. The Write tool stores the bytes verbatim, so
command substitutions or backticks in adversarial diff text are never shell-expanded.
{% if not _is_codex %}
> **Sandbox escape (ADR-003, Claude Code only).** The `codex exec` call needs
> outbound network, which Claude Code's Bash sandbox blocks. Run THIS ONE Bash call
> with the Bash tool parameter **`dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`** — the
> `Bash(codex exec:*)` settings `allow` rule pre-approves the prompt, and Codex stays
> contained by its own `--sandbox read-only` flags. Do NOT disable the sandbox for
> any other command.
{%- else %}
> **Codex runtime note.** The Claude-Code Bash-tool sandbox-escape parameter and the
> `.claude/settings.json` allow rule do NOT apply on the Codex runtime — run the
> `codex exec` call directly and approve it per your Codex sandbox/approval policy.
> Codex stays contained by its own `--sandbox read-only` flags either way.
{%- endif %}
Finally run `codex exec` as its **own** Bash call — the command MUST begin with
`codex exec` so the `Bash(codex exec:*)` allow rule matches it:

```bash
codex exec --sandbox read-only {% if _csoo.get('hermetic', true) %}--ignore-user-config --ignore-rules {% endif %}--output-schema {{ _csoo.get('output_schema_path', '.claude/schemas/codex-finding.schema.json') }} --output-last-message "$out_tmp" - < "$prompt_tmp"; echo "exit=$?"
```

**Adapt (status-guarded).** Only when the `codex exec` exit was 0, pipe the
output file through the adapter (deterministic — it maps severity
`critical→P0 / high→P1 / medium→P2 / low,info→P3`, sets `source: "codex"`, and sets
`needs_relaxation: true` when `file`/`line` is null; reading from the file keeps
untrusted Codex content out of the shell). Always clean up the temp files:

```bash
if [ "$exit" -eq 0 ]; then uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} python -m harness_maker.codex_adapter adapt < "$out_tmp"; fi; rm -f "$prompt_tmp" "$out_tmp"
```

**Skip relay (mandatory surfacing):** on a non-zero `codex exec` exit set
`codex_status: "skipped"` + a one-line `codex_skip_reason`, surface it (do NOT block
— warn-and-proceed), and append a best-effort ledger row. Pass each value as a
**separate `--flag`** (never inline an untrusted cause into a shell-quoted JSON blob
— REVIEW security P1):

```bash
reason="<one-line cause>"; uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} python -m harness_maker.codex_ledger emit --slug "<slug>" --stage {{ _stage }} --finding-ref "n/a" --disposition unresolved --codex-status skipped --skip-reason "$reason"
```

On success set `codex_status: "invoked"`. A silently-degraded Codex is the H4 failure
mode — the `/hm:health` smoke check is the positive backstop.
{%- endif -%}
